{
	"_comment": "Magyar (Hungarian). No grammatical gender — declared by omission, matching German/Dutch's no-agreement shape rather than the Romance agreement locales. Verbs are keyed by the dictionary citation form (3rd person singular present indefinite, e.g. töröl 'deletes' — this IS the Hungarian dictionary headword form, unlike most European languages which cite the infinitive). past = the -va/-ve adverbial participle (törölve 'deleted', the register every real Hungarian app status uses: Mentve, Törölve). gerund = the -ás/-és deverbal noun for progress (törlés → 'Törlés...'), matching real Hungarian software (Mentés..., Telepítés...). Hungarian marks perfective/completed aspect with a PREVERB (el-, meg-, le-, fel-) on the past form where real usage takes one (elküldve 'sent', megnyitva 'opened', leállítva 'stopped') while the bare stem covers the progress noun (küldés 'sending', not elküldés) — this split is genuine Hungarian grammar, not decoration. 'find' is authored as a deliberate cross-verb pair (talál 'find' → past megtalálva 'found', gerund keresés 'searching' from the related verb keres 'search') because that is what real Hungarian software actually shows for an in-progress find. failed_to ('Nem sikerült' = 'did not succeed') wants a following INFINITIVE (törölni) in natural Hungarian; the engine mechanically appends the bare dictionary-form verb instead, so 'fail.push branch' composes 'Nem sikerült felküld ág' — a documented mechanical quirk, the Hungarian analogue of German's telegraphic 'Fehlgeschlagen: pushen Commit'. Tech-specific verbs without one settled Hungarian localisation convention: push→felküld ('send up'), commit→rögzít ('record/fix in place' — solid for the database/transaction sense, less certain for git specifically). Nouns pluralise with -k plus a vowel-harmony linking vowel authored per noun (fájl→fájlok, hiba→hibák); numerals grammatically keep the Hungarian noun singular ('5 fájl', not '5 fájlok') but the engine's CLDR one/other split needs a real 'other' form to prove the mechanism, so count.* deliberately exercises the plural noun table (documented here, not hidden). time.ago is authored separately and correctly: the -e/-a/-ja possessive-temporal suffix (öt perce, két órája) attaches to the SINGULAR stem for every count — there is no plural form of this idiom, so one and other are intentionally identical. The az/a definite split (definite_vowel) is the headline mechanism: az before a vowel-initial word (az adat), a otherwise (a fájl), tested on the letter per docs/locale-schema.md. Known engine limitation, not exercised in tests: the plural-definite shortcut (definite_plural) does not re-run the vowel test, so a vowel-initial plural like 'adatok' would incorrectly get 'a' rather than 'az' — the singular split is fully correct.",
